{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"rg --files .pose/specs | rg 'validate-carries'; pose context --task spec:pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks --json; git diff dec2e64c..8300e10 -- pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run.go\"","aggregated_output":".pose/specs/2026-11-22-pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"selected_project_id\": \"proj.pose-dist\",\n  \"selected_revision\": \"8300e10ef0951010c1ca8ff389b5f6133d0a8b9a\",\n  \"task_ref\": \"xref:proj.pose-dist/spec:pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks\",\n  \"authority\": {\n    \"project_id\": \"proj.pose-dist\",\n    \"kind\": \"spec\",\n    \"slug\": \"pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks\"\n  },\n  \"coordinator_relation\": \"same-project-authority\",\n  \"task_resolution\": {\n    \"schema_version\": 1,\n    \"identity\": {\n      \"project_id\": \"proj.pose-dist\",\n      \"kind\": \"spec\",\n      \"slug\": \"pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks\"\n    },\n    \"resolved\": true,\n    \"resolution_state\": \"resolved\",\n    \"status\": \"in-progress\",\n    \"source_revision\": \"8300e10ef0951010c1ca8ff389b5f6133d0a8b9a\",\n    \"source_digest\": \"e76c9ab14a25fe6f841610a0401a496198f6047dd90d4b6acefb77a463ee253e\",\n    \"dependencies\": [\n      {\n        \"project_id\": \"proj.pose-dist\",\n        \"kind\": \"spec\",\n        \"slug\": \"pose-changed-scope-validation\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"project_id\": \"proj.pose-dist\",\n        \"kind\": \"spec\",\n        \"slug\": \"pose-validation-evidence-material-binding\"\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"authority_revision\": \"8300e10ef0951010c1ca8ff389b5f6133d0a8b9a\",\n  \"supported_contracts\": [\n    \"qualified-artifact-refs@1\",\n    \"spec-authority-transfer@1\",\n    \"federated-roadmap-acceptance@1\"\n  ],\n  \"context_revision\": \"1b2327a7a2f78eaed3ea574fdd714f16d517f6933e882473688b7b213366ca0d\"\n}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go\nindex 1ed4d9a5..977f604e 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go\n@@ -65,7 +65,10 @@ type validationEntry struct {\n \tskip     string\n \tsilent   bool\n \tisolated bool\n-\tresult   checkResult\n+\t// carried marks a check whose result comes from the canonical base (spec\n+\t// pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks R4); it is silent and never runs.\n+\tcarried bool\n+\tresult  checkResult\n \tdone     chan struct{}\n }\n \n@@ -905,6 +908,7 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \tverbose := false\n \tautoReport := false\n \trootOnly := false\n+\tcarry := false\n \tworkspaceFilter := \"\"\n \tjobs := 0\n \tvar isolationChecks []checkResult\n@@ -922,6 +926,10 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \t\t\t// A check's output is captured by default; --verbose streams it\n \t\t\t// (spec pose-cli-output-rendering-system R7).\n \t\t\tverbose = true\n+\t\tcase \"--carry\":\n+\t\t\t// Refresh the canonical result, carrying the checks a change\n+\t\t\t// cannot affect (spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks).\n+\t\t\tcarry = true\n \t\tcase \"--root-only\":\n \t\t\t// Documented alias of --module . (spec\n \t\t\t// pose-monorepo-validation-advisory, R2) — sugar over the\n@@ -995,9 +1003,22 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \t\t}\n \t}\n \tcanonicalResultsPath := \"\"\n-\tif jsonOut != \"\" {\n+\tif jsonOut != \"\" || carry {\n \t\tcanonicalResultsPath = configuredDeliveryResultsPath(root)\n \t}\n+\tif carry {\n+\t\tswitch {\n+\t\tcase moduleFilter != \"\" || stackFilter != \"\" || workspaceFilter != \"\" || changedFrom != \"\" || changedTo != \"\":\n+\t\t\trender(stdout, stderr).Failure(cliText(locale, \"--carry refreshes the whole canonical result; it cannot be combined with --module, --stack, --workspace, --root-only, --changed-from or --changed-to\", \"--carry renova o resultado canônico inteiro; não combina com --module, --stack, --workspace, --root-only, --changed-from ou --changed-to\"))\n+\t\t\treturn 2\n+\t\tcase canonicalResultsPath == \"\":\n+\t\t\trender(stdout, stderr).Failure(cliText(locale, \"--carry needs results_path in .pose/policy/delivery.json\", \"--carry exige results_path em .pose/policy/delivery.json\"))\n+\t\t\treturn 2\n+\t\tcase normalizeValidationOutputPath(jsonOut) != canonicalResultsPath:\n+\t\t\trender(stdout, stderr).Failure(fmt.Sprintf(cliText(locale, \"--carry writes the canonical result: use --json-out %s\", \"--carry grava o resultado canônico: use --json-out %s\"), canonicalResultsPath))\n+\t\t\treturn 2\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n \t// The report --report writes records this run, so it reads this run's own\n \t// output. It used to look for a pose-validate.latest.log that native\n \t// validation never writes, and every report said \"Fill manually\" and \"No\n@@ -1121,6 +1142,28 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \t}\n \tmatrixDigest := sha256.Sum256(raw)\n \trun.MatrixSHA256 = \"sha256:\" + hex.EncodeToString(matrixDigest[:])\n+\trun.EngineVersion = validationEngineIdentity()\n+\tstartedClean := materialWorktreeClean(root)\n+\tvar carryState carryPlan\n+\tif carry {\n+\t\tcarryState = planCarry(root, canonicalResultsPath, mode, run.MatrixSHA256, matrix, modules)\n+\t\trun.Carry = carryState.record\n+\t\tout := render(stdout, stderr)\n+\t\tif !carryState.record.Used {\n+\t\t\tout.ContractLine(\"[carry] full run: \" + carryState.record.Reason)\n+\t\t} else {\n+\t\t\tout.ContractLine(fmt.Sprintf(\"[carry] base %s: %d changed file(s), %d/%d module(s) run\", carryState.record.BaseHead, carryState.record.Changed, len(carryState.record.Selected), len(modules)))\n+\t\t\tif explain {\n+\t\t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n+\t\t\t\t\tif reason, ok := carryState.record.Selected[m.Rel]; ok {\n+\t\t\t\t\t\tout.ContractLine(\"  + \" + m.Rel + \": \" + reason)\n+\t\t\t\t\t} else {\n+\t\t\t\t\t\tout.ContractLine(\"  = \" + m.Rel + \": carried from \" + carryState.record.BaseHead)\n+\t\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n \tif head, err := gitOutputBounded(root, 1024, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"); err == nil {\n \t\trun.GitHead = strings.TrimSpace(string(head))\n \t}\n@@ -1171,6 +1214,9 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \t\t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif carryState.carried(module.Rel) {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tfor _, check := range checks {\n \t\t\tif check.Program != \"\" {\n \t\t\t\tplanned++\n@@ -1214,6 +1260,16 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \t\t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif carryState.carried(module.Rel) {\n+\t\t\tfor _, check := range checks {\n+\t\t\t\tif check.Program == \"\" {\n+\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t\tid := module.Rel + \"/\" + stack + \"/\" + check.Name\n+\t\t\t\tentries = append(entries, &validationEntry{check: check, silent: true, carried: true, result: carryState.carriedResult(id)})\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tmoduleMode := mode\n \t\tif override.Mode != \"\" {\n \t\t\tmoduleMode = override.Mode\n@@ -1269,6 +1325,34 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \t\t\tsteps.Note(entry.note)\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif entry.carried {\n+\t\t\t// A carried result counts toward the outcome exactly as it did in\n+\t\t\t// its own run; a carried failure stays a failure (R4).\n+\t\t\trun.Counts.Carried++\n+\t\t\tswitch entry.result.Outcome {\n+\t\t\tcase \"pass\":\n+\t\t\t\trun.Counts.Passed++\n+\t\t\tcase \"fail\":\n+\t\t\t\tif entry.result.Severity == \"required\" {\n+\t\t\t\t\tfailures++\n+\t\t\t\t\trun.Counts.Failed++\n+\t\t\t\t} else {\n+\t\t\t\t\toptionalFailures++\n+\t\t\t\t\trun.Counts.OptionalFailed++\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tcase \"skipped\":\n+\t\t\t\trun.Counts.Skipped++\n+\t\t\tdefault:\n+\t\t\t\trun.Counts.Errored++\n+\t\t\t\tif entry.result.Severity == \"required\" {\n+\t\t\t\t\tfailures++\n+\t\t\t\t} else {\n+\t\t\t\t\toptionalFailures++\n+\t\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\trun.Checks = append(run.Checks, entry.result)\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n \t\tif !entry.silent && !entry.isolated {\n \t\t\tif jobs > 1 {\n \t\t\t\t<-entry.done\n@@ -1335,7 +1419,9 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \trun.WallSeconds = time.Since(wallStart).Seconds()\n \t// A check that changed a tracked file changed the material it ran over: the\n \t// committed tree no longer describes what was validated.\n-\tif len(run.MaterialProvenance) > 0 && !materialTrackedUnchanged(root) {\n+\tendedClean := materialTrackedUnchanged(root)\n+\trun.MaterialClean = startedClean && endedClean\n+\tif len(run.MaterialProvenance) > 0 && !endedClean {\n \t\tfor spec := range run.MaterialProvenance {\n \t\t\trun.MaterialProvenance[spec] = posemodel.MaterialBindingUnavailable\n \t\t}\n@@ -1350,7 +1436,7 @@ func cmdValidateWithStore(store posemodel.Store, args []string, stdout, stderr i\n \t\tsteps.Note(line)\n \t}\n \tsteps.Summary()\n-\tif executed == 0 {\n+\tif executed == 0 && run.Counts.Carried == 0 {\n \t\tfmt.Fprintln(stdout, \"No modules/checks matched the matrix and filters.\")\n \t}\n \tresult := \"pass\"\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 00000000..9356cb01\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,191 @@\n+package cli\n+\n+// Carried checks (spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks): with a\n+// comparable canonical result as base, validate --carry runs the modules the\n+// changed scope selects between the base head and HEAD and carries the base\n+// results of every other module, each stating the head it ran at. Any doubt\n+// about the base runs everything and records why (R2).\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"encoding/json\"\n+\t\"fmt\"\n+\t\"os\"\n+\t\"path/filepath\"\n+\t\"runtime/debug\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\n+\tposemodel \"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n+\t\"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/version\"\n+)\n+\n+// carryRecord is the run's account of its base (R5).\n+type carryRecord struct {\n+\tUsed     bool              `json:\"used\"`\n+\tReason   string            `json:\"reason,omitempty\"`\n+\tBaseHead string            `json:\"base_head,omitempty\"`\n+\tChanged  int               `json:\"changed\"`\n+\tSelected map[string]string `json:\"selected,omitempty\"`\n+}\n+\n+// carryPlan is what a usable base lets the run reuse.\n+type carryPlan struct {\n+\trecord    *carryRecord\n+\tselection *scopeSelection\n+\tbase      map[string]checkResult\n+\tbaseHead  string\n+\tbaseTime  string\n+}\n+\n+// carried reports whether module's checks come from the base.\n+func (p carryPlan) carried(module string) bool {\n+\tif p.selection == nil {\n+\t\treturn false\n+\t}\n+\t_, selected := p.selection.Selected[module]\n+\treturn !selected\n+}\n+\n+// carriedResult is the base result of a check as this run records it (R4):\n+// unchanged, plus where and when it really ran, kept through chained carries.\n+func (p carryPlan) carriedResult(id string) checkResult {\n+\tresult := p.base[id]\n+\tif result.CarriedFrom == \"\" {\n+\t\tresult.CarriedFrom, result.CarriedGeneratedAt = p.baseHead, p.baseTime\n+\t}\n+\treturn result\n+}\n+\n+// planCarry judges the canonical result at resultsPath as a base. A nil\n+// selection means every check runs; the record says why.\n+func planCarry(root, resultsPath, mode, matrixSHA string, matrix validationMatrix, modules []validationModule) carryPlan {\n+\tplan := carryPlan{record: &carryRecord{}}\n+\tunused := func(format string, args ...any) carryPlan {\n+\t\tplan.record.Reason = fmt.Sprintf(format, args...)\n+\t\treturn plan\n+\t}\n+\traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(root, filepath.FromSlash(resultsPath)))\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn unused(\"no canonical result at %s\", resultsPath)\n+\t}\n+\tvar base validationRunResult\n+\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &base); err != nil || base.SchemaVersion != validationResultSchema {\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result is unreadable or has another schema\")\n+\t}\n+\tswitch {\n+\tcase base.ModuleFilter != \"\" || base.StackFilter != \"\":\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result was filtered by module or stack, so it is not comparable\")\n+\tcase base.Mode != mode:\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result ran in mode %q, this run is %q\", base.Mode, mode)\n+\tcase base.MatrixSHA256 != matrixSHA:\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the validation matrix changed since the canonical result\")\n+\tcase base.EngineVersion == \"\" || base.EngineVersion != validationEngineIdentity():\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the engine version differs from the canonical result's or was not recorded\")\n+\tcase base.GitHead == \"\" || !safeGitRevRE.MatchString(base.GitHead):\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result records no usable head\")\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, check := range base.Checks {\n+\t\tif check.LimitState == \"cancelled\" {\n+\t\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result was cancelled\")\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\t// The base must have validated the commit it records: a run over a working\n+\t// copy that differed from its head proves nothing about that head.\n+\tif !base.MaterialClean {\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result did not validate a clean working copy of its head\")\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, binding := range base.MaterialProvenance {\n+\t\tif binding == posemodel.MaterialBindingUnavailable {\n+\t\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result records an unavailable material binding\")\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif !materialWorktreeClean(root) {\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the working copy differs from HEAD in material paths\")\n+\t}\n+\tif err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", root, \"merge-base\", \"--is-ancestor\", base.GitHead, \"HEAD\").Run(); err != nil {\n+\t\treturn unused(\"the canonical result's head %s is not an ancestor of HEAD\", base.GitHead)\n+\t}\n+\tselection, err := computeChangedScope(root, base.GitHead, \"HEAD\", modules, carryExclusions(root, base.GitHead, resultsPath)...)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn unused(\"changed-scope selection failed: %v\", err)\n+\t}\n+\tplan.base = map[string]checkResult{}\n+\tfor _, check := range base.Checks {\n+\t\tplan.base[check.ID] = check\n+\t}\n+\t// A module whose checks are not all in the base runs (R3).\n+\tfor _, module := range modules {\n+\t\tif _, selected := selection.Selected[module.Rel]; selected {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tstack, checks, _ := moduleValidationChecks(module, matrix, \"\", \"\")\n+\t\tfor _, check := range checks {\n+\t\t\tif check.Program == \"\" {\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t\tid := module.Rel + \"/\" + stack + \"/\" + check.Name\n+\t\t\tif prior, ok := plan.base[id]; !ok || prior.Outcome == \"skipped\" || prior.Outcome == \"\" {\n+\t\t\t\tselection.Selected[module.Rel] = \"carry: no prior result for \" + id\n+\t\t\t\tbreak\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tplan.selection, plan.baseHead, plan.baseTime = &selection, base.GitHead, base.GeneratedAt\n+\tplan.record.Used, plan.record.BaseHead, plan.record.Changed, plan.record.Selected = true, base.GitHead, len(selection.Changed), selection.Selected\n+\treturn plan\n+}\n+\n+// carryExclusions are the changed paths that are not changes for the carry\n+// selection (R3): the canonical result itself and the derived administrative\n+// records of the engine's closed list (the same classes material bindings\n+// ignore) that are still regular files, or gone, at HEAD. A symlink,\n+// submodule or directory in a class stays a change.\n+func carryExclusions(root, base, resultsPath string) []string {\n+\texclude := []string{resultsPath}\n+\tout, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", root, \"diff\", \"--name-only\", \"-z\", base, \"HEAD\", \"--\").Output()\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\treturn exclude\n+\t}\n+\tfor _, file := range strings.Split(string(out), \"\\x00\") {\n+\t\tfile = filepath.ToSlash(file)\n+\t\tif file == \"\" || !posemodel.IsAdministrativePath(file) {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tentry, err := posemodel.RawGit(\"-C\", root, \"ls-tree\", \"-z\", \"HEAD\", \"--\", file).Output()\n+\t\tif err != nil {\n+\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tfields := strings.Fields(string(entry))\n+\t\tif len(fields) == 0 || fields[0] == \"100644\" || fields[0] == \"100755\" {\n+\t\t\texclude = append(exclude, file)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn exclude\n+}\n+\n+// validationEngineIdentity names the engine that ran a validation: its version\n+// plus, when the binary records it, the VCS revision it was built from, marked\n+// when the build tree was modified. Development builds share a version string,\n+// so the revision is what tells two of them apart (R2).\n+func validationEngineIdentity() string {\n+\tidentity := version.Version\n+\tinfo, ok := debug.ReadBuildInfo()\n+\tif !ok {\n+\t\treturn identity\n+\t}\n+\trevision, modified := \"\", false\n+\tfor _, setting := range info.Settings {\n+\t\tswitch setting.Key {\n+\t\tcase \"vcs.revision\":\n+\t\t\trevision = setting.Value\n+\t\tcase \"vcs.modified\":\n+\t\t\tmodified = setting.Value == \"true\"\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif revision != \"\" {\n+\t\tidentity += \"+\" + revision\n+\t\tif modified {\n+\t\t\tidentity += \".dirty\"\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn identity\n+}\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go\nindex 6af16e09..c862a8af 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go\n@@ -42,6 +42,10 @@ type checkResult struct {\n \t// Runtime guardrails (spec pose-validation-runtime-guardrails), additive:\n \tLimitState string `json:\"limit_state,omitempty\"` // timeout | output-limit | cancelled | no-tests\n \tIsolation  string `json:\"isolation,omitempty\"`   // required = delegated to Harness\n+\t// A carried result is the base's, run at CarriedFrom (spec\n+\t// pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks R4).\n+\tCarriedFrom        string `json:\"carried_from,omitempty\"`\n+\tCarriedGeneratedAt string `json:\"carried_generated_at,omitempty\"`\n }\n \n // outputLimiter cancels the check when total output exceeds the limit —\n@@ -73,6 +77,14 @@ type validationRunResult struct {\n \tProvenanceDigest   string            `json:\"provenance_digest,omitempty\"`\n \tScopeProvenance    map[string]string `json:\"scope_provenance,omitempty\"`\n \tMaterialProvenance map[string]string `json:\"material_provenance,omitempty\"`\n+\t// EngineVersion and Carry let a later run judge this one as a base (spec\n+\t// pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks R2, R5).\n+\tEngineVersion string       `json:\"engine_version,omitempty\"`\n+\tCarry         *carryRecord `json:\"carry,omitempty\"`\n+\t// MaterialClean says the working copy matched HEAD in material paths when\n+\t// the run started and still did when it ended, so the run validated the\n+\t// commit it records. Only such a run can be a carry base (R2).\n+\tMaterialClean bool `json:\"material_clean,omitempty\"`\n \t// Jobs is how many checks ran at once; WallSeconds the run's elapsed time\n \t// (spec pose-validate-concurrent-steps R3–R4).\n \tJobs        int     `json:\"jobs,omitempty\"`\n@@ -91,6 +103,7 @@ type runCounts struct {\n \tOptionalFailed int `json:\"optional_failed\"`\n \tErrored        int `json:\"errored\"`\n \tSkipped        int `json:\"skipped\"`\n+\tCarried        int `json:\"carried,omitempty\"`\n }\n \n // secretEnvKeyRE marks configured env keys whose values are redacted from\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go\nindex 841014bf..05e582c2 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go\n@@ -25,6 +25,10 @@ var safeGitRevRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._/~^-]*$`)\n type scopeModuleMeta struct {\n \tCriticality string   `json:\"criticality\"`\n \tDependsOn   []string `json:\"dependsOn\"`\n+\t// SelfContained declares that the module's checks read only its own\n+\t// directory and its declared dependencies, so a change outside every\n+\t// module does not select it (spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks R6).\n+\tSelfContained bool `json:\"selfContained\"`\n }\n \n type scopeMetadata struct {\n@@ -57,8 +61,10 @@ func (s scopeSelection) rangeLabel() string {\n // computeChangedScope resolves the selection. Modules are selected when they\n // contain changed files, depend (transitively) on a changed module, or policy\n // widens them (criticality high). A change outside every module selects\n-// everything — uncertainty prefers safe execution.\n-func computeChangedScope(root, base, head string, modules []validationModule) (scopeSelection, error) {\n+// every module that does not declare itself self-contained — uncertainty\n+// prefers safe execution. Excluded paths (the run's own canonical result) are\n+// not changes.\n+func computeChangedScope(root, base, head string, modules []validationModule, exclude ...string) (scopeSelection, error) {\n \tsel := scopeSelection{Base: base, Head: head, Selected: map[string]string{}}\n \tfor _, rev := range []string{base, head} {\n \t\tif rev != \"\" && !safeGitRevRE.MatchString(rev) {\n@@ -90,6 +96,19 @@ func computeChangedScope(root, base, head string, modules []validationModule) (s\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\n \t}\n+\tif len(exclude) > 0 {\n+\t\texcluded := map[string]bool{}\n+\t\tfor _, path := range exclude {\n+\t\t\texcluded[filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path))] = true\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tkept := sel.Changed[:0]\n+\t\tfor _, file := range sel.Changed {\n+\t\t\tif !excluded[file] {\n+\t\t\t\tkept = append(kept, file)\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tsel.Changed = kept\n+\t}\n \tsort.Strings(sel.Changed)\n \n \tmeta := loadScopeMetadata(root)\n@@ -110,13 +129,16 @@ func computeChangedScope(root, base, head string, modules []validationModule) (s\n \t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m] = \"contains changed file: \" + file\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t} else {\n-\t\t\t// Root-level or unmapped change: safe execution wins.\n+\t\t\t// Root-level or unmapped change: safe execution wins, except for\n+\t\t\t// modules that declare their checks read nothing outside them.\n \t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n+\t\t\t\tif meta.Modules[m.Rel].SelfContained {\n+\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t\t}\n \t\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; !ok {\n \t\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m.Rel] = \"root-level change outside any module: \" + file\n \t\t\t\t}\n \t\t\t}\n-\t\t\treturn sel, nil\n \t\t}\n \t}\n \t// Policy widening: high-criticality modules always run.\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run.go\nindex 07b52e0b..f78cddd7 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run.go\n@@ -28,6 +28,10 @@ func ParseDeliveryValidationRun(raw []byte, relative string) ([]DeliveryValidati\n \t\t\tSeverity      string `json:\"severity\"`\n \t\t\tEvidenceClass string `json:\"evidence_class,omitempty\"`\n \t\t\tOutcome       string `json:\"outcome\"`\n+\t\t\t// A carried check ran at its own head (spec\n+\t\t\t// pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks R7).\n+\t\t\tCarriedFrom        string `json:\"carried_from,omitempty\"`\n+\t\t\tCarriedGeneratedAt string `json:\"carried_generated_at,omitempty\"`\n \t\t} `json:\"checks\"`\n \t}\n \tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &run); err != nil || run.SchemaVersion != DeliveryValidationResultSchema {\n@@ -38,7 +42,11 @@ func ParseDeliveryValidationRun(raw []byte, relative string) ([]DeliveryValidati\n \t\tif check.EvidenceClass == \"\" {\n \t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t}\n-\t\tresults = append(results, DeliveryValidationResult{ID: check.ID, Module: check.Module, Check: check.Name, EvidenceClass: check.EvidenceClass, Severity: check.Severity, Outcome: check.Outcome, GitHead: run.GitHead, GeneratedAt: run.GeneratedAt, ProvenanceDigest: run.ProvenanceDigest, ScopeProvenance: run.ScopeProvenance, MaterialProvenance: run.MaterialProvenance, Report: relative})\n+\t\thead, generated := run.GitHead, run.GeneratedAt\n+\t\tif check.CarriedFrom != \"\" {\n+\t\t\thead, generated = check.CarriedFrom, check.CarriedGeneratedAt\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tresults = append(results, DeliveryValidationResult{ID: check.ID, Module: check.Module, Check: check.Name, EvidenceClass: check.EvidenceClass, Severity: check.Severity, Outcome: check.Outcome, GitHead: head, GeneratedAt: generated, ProvenanceDigest: run.ProvenanceDigest, ScopeProvenance: run.ScopeProvenance, MaterialProvenance: run.MaterialProvenance, Report: relative})\n \t}\n \tsort.Slice(results, func(i, j int) bool { return results[i].ID < results[j].ID })\n \treturn results, nil\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/specs/2026-11-22-pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc 'cat .pose/specs/2026-11-22-pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md; cat pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go'","aggregated_output":"---\nslug: pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks\nstatus: in-progress\ncreated_at: 2026-11-22\ncompleted_at:\nsupersedes:\ndepends_on: pose-changed-scope-validation, pose-validation-evidence-material-binding\nremediates:\npriority: 1\ncomponents: pose-mcp\ntask_type: feature\nsurface: standard\ndelivers:\n---\n\n# Spec: pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks\n\n## 1. Intent\n\n### Goal\nLet `pose validate` refresh the canonical structured result by running only the checks a change can affect and carrying forward, unchanged and labelled, the results of the checks it cannot affect.\n\n### Business value\nIn the Harne8 batch of 2026-11-22, every material change to one module required the whole 72-check matrix again. Each run took about 35 minutes with three jobs. Intermittent failures in unrelated suites made roughly one run in three useless. A hooks change confined to `scripts/` needed four full runs in one review round. The canonical record is all-or-nothing today. `--changed-from` already selects the affected modules, but it writes a partial record that cannot replace the canonical one in `results_path`, so closeout still needs the full run.\n\n### Constraints\nA carried result must say where it ran. It never upgrades its outcome, so a carried failure stays a failure. Any doubt about eligibility widens to a full run, and the record states why. The selection reuses the changed-scope rules and widens them only by explicit declaration. Results and indexes written by earlier engines keep their meaning.\n\n### Non-goals\nCaching individual command outputs, hashing arbitrary inputs of a check, carrying across a matrix or engine change, and changing how review bundles seal evidence beyond reading the per-check head.\n\n## 2. Requirements\n\n### Functional\n- R1: `pose validate --carry --json-out <results_path>` shall read the canonical record at the configured `results_path` as its base. It shall be refused with exit 2 when no `results_path` is configured, when the output path is not that path, or when combined with `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--changed-from` or `--changed-to`.\n- R2: The base shall be used only when every condition below holds. Otherwise the run shall execute every check and record `carry.used: false` with the first failing condition as `carry.reason`.\n  - Its schema, mode, `matrix_sha256` and `engine_version` equal the current run's.\n  - Its `git_head` is an ancestor of, or equal to, `HEAD`.\n  - It was not cancelled.\n  - It recorded `material_clean`: its own working copy matched its head when it started and when it ended, and none of its material bindings is unavailable.\n  - The working copy is clean in material paths.\n- R3: With a usable base, the run shall select modules with the changed-scope rules over `base.git_head..HEAD`, excluding the `results_path` file itself. It shall also exclude derived administrative records of the engine's closed list (`posemodel.IsAdministrativePath`, the classes material bindings ignore) that are regular files, or absent, at `HEAD`. Otherwise the index and state commits that follow every record would select every module. It shall execute every check of a selected module. Every check of an unselected module whose base result exists and was not skipped shall be carried. A module with any check lacking such a base result shall be selected with the reason `carry: no prior result for <check id>`.\n- R4: A carried check shall keep the base result's outcome, exit code, duration, limit state, output and evidence class. It shall add `carried_from`, the head the check actually ran at, preserved through chained carries, and `carried_generated_at`. Counts shall add `carried`. Carried failures and errors shall count toward the run outcome exactly like executed ones.\n- R5: The record shall add `engine_version` on every run: the engine version plus the VCS revision the binary was built from, when the build records it. With `--carry` it shall also add a `carry` object: `used`, `reason`, `base_head`, `changed` (the number of files) and `selected` (module to reason).\n- R6: A module declared `\"selfContained\": true` in `.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json` shall not be selected by a change outside every module. It is still selected by changes inside it, by its declared `dependsOn` and by policy. This shall hold for `--changed-from` and `--carry` alike, with the reason recorded for the modules it widens.\n- R7: Reading the record into the delivery index shall use a check's `carried_from` and `carried_generated_at` as that result's head and time. Every consumer of a result's head therefore judges the commit a carried check really ran at.\n\n### Non-functional\n- R8: A run where every module is carried shall execute no check and finish in the time of the selection and the record write.\n\n### Security\n- R9: Revisions read from the base record shall pass the same confinement as `--changed-from` revisions before any Git call.\n\n### Compatibility\n- R10: A base record without `engine_version` shall never be used. Records without the new fields shall be read exactly as before.\n\n## 3. Technical Plan\n\n### Affected areas\n`pose-mcp/internal/cli` (argument parsing, the run loop and record fields of `validate`, changed-scope selection), `pose-mcp/internal/pose` (reading the structured result into delivery results), the manual and the CLI help.\n\n### Artifacts\n- created: .pose/specs/2026-11-22-pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md\n- created: .pose/adr/2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_results.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry_test.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run.go\n- created: pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_validation_run_test.go\n- modified: POSE.md\n- modified: locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/cli/help_catalog.go\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/POSE.md\n- modified: pose-mcp/internal/scaffold/dist/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n\n### Delivery targets\n\n### API/contract changes\nAdditive fields in the structured validation result: `engine_version`, `carry`, `counts.carried`, and per check `carried_from` and `carried_generated_at`. There is a new `validate --carry` flag and a new optional `selfContained` field in module metadata.\n\n### Data/storage changes\nNone beyond the additive record fields.\n\n### Technical risks\nAn instance that marks a module `selfContained` while its checks read root files would carry stale results. The declaration is explicit, documented and opt-in, and the default stays the full fallback.\n\n## 4. Tasks\n\n### Planning\n- [x] ADR for carrying results and the self-contained declaration.\n\n### Implementation\n- [x] Record fields and base eligibility.\n- [x] Selection excluding the results file, the self-contained declaration and the no-prior-result widening.\n- [x] Carried entries in the run loop and counts.\n- [x] Per-check head in the delivery reading.\n- [x] Manual and help.\n\n### Validation\n- [x] Unit and fixture tests for every requirement, each shown failing before its code.\n- [x] `pose validate --module pose-mcp` and `check --strict`.\n\n## 5. Decisions\n\n### Decision D1\nA carried result is the base's result, not a new observation. It keeps the head where it ran, and consumers judge that head. The run's material binding is still computed at `HEAD`. This holds only because the selection guarantees that a carried module's inputs did not change between the two heads, under the changed-scope rules and the instance's declarations. See ADR `2026-11-22-validate-carries-unchanged-checks`.\n\n### Decision D2\nEligibility compares the whole matrix digest and the engine version instead of per-check definitions. A matrix or engine change reruns everything. That is the existing rule for material bindings, and it keeps the comparison obviously sound.\n\n### Decision D3\nOut-of-module changes keep selecting every module unless a module declares itself self-contained. Instances opt in per module, so a repository that never declares anything gets today's behaviour.\n\n## 6. Validation\n\n### Strategy\nGo tests in `internal/cli` drive real Git fixtures through `validate --carry`. They cover:\n- the base conditions of R2, one fixture each;\n- a change in one module that carries the other;\n- chained carries;\n- a carried failure;\n- the self-contained declaration of R6, under both flags;\n- the refusals of R1;\n- the per-check head reading of R7.\n\nEach test is run against the code before its requirement and must fail there.\n\n### Deterministic checks\n\n#### Test\n- Command: `go test ./internal/cli/ ./internal/pose/` (in `pose-mcp`), then `pose validate --module pose-mcp`.\n\n#### Lint\n- Command: `go vet ./...` (in `pose-mcp`).\n\n#### Typecheck\n- Command: compiled by the tests.\n\n#### Build\n- Command: `go build ./...` (in `pose-mcp`).\n\n#### Security / Contract\n- Command: `pose check --strict`.\n\n### Execution log\n- 2026-11-22: the new fixtures in `validate_carry_test.go` and `delivery_validation_run_test.go` drive real Git repositories through `validate --carry`. Each requirement's code was mutated out and its test failed: the per-check head reading, the self-contained declaration, the canonical-result exclusion and the derived-record exclusion.\n- 2026-11-22: `go test ./...` in `pose-mcp` first failed on `TestDirectPrintSitesOnlyShrink` and `TestEmbeddedDistMatchesPoseDist`; the carry lines now go through `cliout` and the embedded manual was regenerated. A full `pose validate` then failed the post-install gate because the manual cited derived-record directories absent from a fresh install; the text was rewritten without those paths.\n- 2026-11-22: `pose validate --json-out .pose/results/delivery-validation.json` with a binary built from `606a0fa8` passed 236 of 236 checks in 786 s.\n- 2026-11-22: dogfood: `validate --carry --explain` at `c4182727` used that record as base. The record and index commits counted as zero changed files. Five of six modules were carried, and `pose-mcp` ran because its criticality is high. The run passed with `counts.carried` 3, and the record is kept at `.pose/bin/batch-20261118/engine-a-dogfood-carried.json` in the Harne8 batch.\n\n- 2026-11-22: review round 1 (rrn-0dd98c4aab8b5918, Codex) found F1 (high). A base that validated a dirty working copy could be carried. The record now has `material_clean`, and a base without it, or with an unavailable material binding, is refused. `TestCarryRefusesABaseThatValidatedADirtyWorkingCopy` reproduces the sequence. It failed with the check mutated out and passes with it.\n\n### Results summary\nAll new and existing tests pass, and the strict matrix is green. In this repository, `pose-mcp` holds 233 of the 236 checks and is high criticality, so carrying saves little here. In an instance with many modules, a change confined to one of them reruns only that module and the modules that are not declared self-contained.\n\n### Requirement trace\n- R1 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRefusesIncompatibleFlags\n- R2 [satisfied] test:TestCarryFallsBackToAFullRun test:TestCarryRefusesAFilteredBase test:TestCarryRefusesABaseThatValidatedADirtyWorkingCopy\n- R3 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRunsOnlyTheChangedModule test:TestCarryRecordFileIsNotAChange test:TestCarryIgnoresDerivedAdministrativeRecords test:TestCarrySelectsAModuleWithoutAPriorResult\n- R4 [satisfied] test:TestCarryKeepsTheOriginalHeadAcrossChainedCarries test:TestCarryKeepsACarriedFailureAFailure\n- R5 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRunsOnlyTheChangedModule\n- R6 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRootChangeRunsEverythingUnlessSelfContained test:TestChangedScopeHonoursSelfContainedModules\n- R7 [satisfied] test:TestDeliveryValidationRunReadsTheCarriedHead\n- R8 [satisfied] test:TestCarryRecordFileIsNotAChange\n- R9 [satisfied] test:TestCarryFallsBackToAFullRun\n- R10 [satisfied] test:TestCarryFallsBackToAFullRun\n\nR9 is enforced by `safeGitRevRE` in `planCarry` before any Git call; the not-an-ancestor case drives a forged head through it. R10 is the `base without engine version` case.\n\n### Known gaps\nReview bundles and closeout consume the per-check head through the delivery index. No separate end-to-end test seals a bundle over a carried record.\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\n### Delivered scope\n`pose validate --carry` refreshes the canonical structured result. It runs the modules the changed-scope rules select since the canonical base and carries the other checks with their outcome and the head where they ran. The changed set ignores the result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings already ignore. Any doubt about the base runs everything, and the record says why. Modules can declare `selfContained` to stop being widened by changes outside every module. The delivery index reads a carried result as an observation of its own head. The record now names the engine version together with its build revision.\n\n### Files and modules changed\n`pose-mcp/internal/cli` (validate, its scope selection, its result fields and the help catalogue), `pose-mcp/internal/pose` (the delivery reading of the result), the manual in English and pt-BR with their embedded copies, this spec and its ADR.\n\n### Validation executed\nSee the execution log: the package tests with mutation checks, the full `go test ./...` and the strict matrix with 236 of 236 checks.\n\n### Residual risks\nAn instance that declares a module self-contained while its checks read files outside it would carry a stale result. The declaration is explicit and documented, and the default stays the full fallback.\n\n### Follow-ups\n- [open] F1: cover with an end-to-end test that a review bundle sealed over a carried record judges the carried head (seal, verify and closeout-check on a fixture). (owner:@pose-maintainers crit:low review:2026-12-15)\npackage cli\n\n// Carried checks (spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks): validate\n// --carry refreshes the canonical result by running only what a change can\n// affect and carrying the rest with the head it really ran at.\n\nimport (\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n)\n\nconst carryResults = \".pose/results/delivery-validation.json\"\n\ntype carryFixture struct {\n\tt       *testing.T\n\troot    string\n\tcounter string\n}\n\n// newCarryFixture builds a committed repository with Go modules a and b and\n// a root file. Every check appends its module to a counter file outside the\n// tree, so a test can tell an executed check from a carried one.\nfunc newCarryFixture(t *testing.T, metadata string) *carryFixture {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tf := &carryFixture{t: t, root: t.TempDir(), counter: filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"runs\")}\n\tf.write(\"a/go.mod\", \"module a\\n\")\n\tf.write(\"b/go.mod\", \"module b\\n\")\n\tf.write(\"root.txt\", \"root\\n\")\n\tf.write(\".gitignore\", \".pose/transfers/.authority-transfer.lock\\n\")\n\tf.write(\".pose/policy/delivery.json\", `{\"schema_version\":1,\"enabled\":true,\"results_path\":\"`+carryResults+`\"}`)\n\tmatrix := `{\"defaults\":{\"mode\":\"strict\"},\"stacks\":{\"go\":{\"checks\":[` +\n\t\t`{\"name\":\"ok\",\"program\":\"sh\",\"args\":[\"-c\",\"basename \\\"$PWD\\\" >> ` + f.counter + `; test ! -f fail\"],\"severity\":\"required\",\"evidenceClass\":\"unit\"}]}}}`\n\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\", matrix)\n\tif metadata != \"\" {\n\t\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/module-metadata.json\", metadata)\n\t}\n\tf.git(\"init\", \"-q\")\n\tf.commit(\"base\")\n\treturn f\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) write(rel, content string) {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tpath := filepath.Join(f.root, rel)\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(filepath.Dir(path), 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif err := os.WriteFile(path, []byte(content), 0o644); err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) git(args ...string) string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tcmd := exec.Command(\"git\", append([]string{\"-C\", f.root}, args...)...)\n\tcmd.Env = append(os.Environ(), \"GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=t\", \"GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=t@t\", \"GIT_COMMITTER_NAME=t\", \"GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL=t@t\", \"GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null\")\n\tout, err := cmd.CombinedOutput()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tf.t.Fatalf(\"git %v: %v %s\", args, err, out)\n\t}\n\treturn strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) commit(message string) string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tf.git(\"add\", \"-A\")\n\tf.git(\"-c\", \"core.hooksPath=/dev/null\", \"commit\", \"-qm\", message, \"--allow-empty\")\n\treturn f.git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\")\n}\n\n// validate runs pose validate and returns its exit code, output and the\n// canonical record it wrote. The counter is reset first.\nfunc (f *carryFixture) validate(args ...string) (int, string, validationRunResult) {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\t_ = os.Remove(f.counter)\n\tcode, out := runValidate(f.t, f.root, append(args, \"--json-out\", carryResults)...)\n\tvar run validationRunResult\n\tif raw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(f.root, carryResults)); err == nil {\n\t\tif err := json.Unmarshal(raw, &run); err != nil {\n\t\t\tf.t.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn code, out, run\n}\n\nfunc (f *carryFixture) ran() []string {\n\traw, err := os.ReadFile(f.counter)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil\n\t}\n\truns := strings.Fields(string(raw))\n\tsort.Strings(runs)\n\treturn runs\n}\n\nfunc checkOf(run validationRunResult, module string) checkResult {\n\tfor _, c := range run.Checks {\n\t\tif c.Module == module {\n\t\t\treturn c\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn checkResult{}\n}\n\n// baseline runs the full matrix, commits the canonical record and returns the\n// head the record validated.\nfunc (f *carryFixture) baseline() string {\n\tf.t.Helper()\n\tvalidated := f.git(\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\")\n\tcode, out, _ := f.validate()\n\tif code != 0 {\n\t\tf.t.Fatalf(\"baseline exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tf.commit(\"record\")\n\treturn validated\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryRunsOnlyTheChangedModule(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tbase := f.baseline()\n\tf.write(\"a/changed.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\thead := f.commit(\"change a\")\n\tcode, out, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"executed modules = %q, want only a\", got)\n\t}\n\tif run.Carry == nil || !run.Carry.Used || run.Carry.BaseHead != base {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"carry = %+v, want used from %s\", run.Carry, base)\n\t}\n\tif b := checkOf(run, \"b\"); b.Outcome != \"pass\" || b.CarriedFrom != base || b.CarriedGeneratedAt == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"b = %+v, want a pass carried from %s\", b, base)\n\t}\n\tif a := checkOf(run, \"a\"); a.CarriedFrom != \"\" || a.Outcome != \"pass\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a = %+v, want executed\", a)\n\t}\n\tif run.GitHead != head || run.Counts.Carried != 1 || run.Counts.Executed != 1 || run.Counts.Passed != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"head=%s counts=%+v\", run.GitHead, run.Counts)\n\t}\n\tif run.EngineVersion == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"engine_version must be recorded\")\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryKeepsTheOriginalHeadAcrossChainedCarries(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tbase := f.baseline()\n\tf.write(\"a/one.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"change a\")\n\tif code, out, _ := f.validate(\"--carry\"); code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tf.commit(\"record again\")\n\tf.write(\"a/two.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"change a again\")\n\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif b := checkOf(run, \"b\"); b.CarriedFrom != base {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"b carried from %q, want the head it ran at %s\", b.CarriedFrom, base)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryKeepsACarriedFailureAFailure(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tf.write(\"b/fail\", \"x\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"b fails\")\n\tif code, _, _ := f.validate(); code == 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"baseline with a failing b must fail\")\n\t}\n\tf.commit(\"record\")\n\tf.write(\"a/changed.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"change a\")\n\tcode, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif code == 0 || run.Outcome != \"fail\" || checkOf(run, \"b\").Outcome != \"fail\" || checkOf(run, \"b\").CarriedFrom == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d outcome=%s b=%+v, want the carried failure to fail the run\", code, run.Outcome, checkOf(run, \"b\"))\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryFallsBackToAFullRun(t *testing.T) {\n\tcases := []struct {\n\t\tname   string\n\t\tmutate func(f *carryFixture)\n\t\treason string\n\t}{\n\t\t{\"no base\", func(f *carryFixture) {}, \"no canonical result\"},\n\t\t{\"matrix changed\", func(f *carryFixture) {\n\t\t\tf.baseline()\n\t\t\traw, _ := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(f.root, \".pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\"))\n\t\t\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/validation-matrix.json\", strings.Replace(string(raw), `\"strict\"`, `\"strict\" `, 1))\n\t\t\tf.commit(\"matrix\")\n\t\t}, \"matrix\"},\n\t\t{\"dirty worktree\", func(f *carryFixture) {\n\t\t\tf.baseline()\n\t\t\tf.write(\"a/dirty.go\", \"package a\\n\")\n\t\t}, \"working copy\"},\n\t\t{\"base not an ancestor\", func(f *carryFixture) {\n\t\t\tf.baseline()\n\t\t\traw, _ := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(f.root, carryResults))\n\t\t\tvar run map[string]any\n\t\t\t_ = json.Unmarshal(raw, &run)\n\t\t\trun[\"git_head\"] = strings.Repeat(\"0\", 40)\n\t\t\tout, _ := json.Marshal(run)\n\t\t\tf.write(carryResults, string(out))\n\t\t\tf.commit(\"forged base\")\n\t\t}, \"ancestor\"},\n\t\t{\"base without engine version\", func(f *carryFixture) {\n\t\t\tf.baseline()\n\t\t\traw, _ := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(f.root, carryResults))\n\t\t\tvar run map[string]any\n\t\t\t_ = json.Unmarshal(raw, &run)\n\t\t\tdelete(run, \"engine_version\")\n\t\t\tout, _ := json.Marshal(run)\n\t\t\tf.write(carryResults, string(out))\n\t\t\tf.commit(\"old base\")\n\t\t}, \"engine\"},\n\t}\n\tfor _, tc := range cases {\n\t\tt.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {\n\t\t\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\t\t\ttc.mutate(f)\n\t\t\t_, out, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\t\t\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a,b\" {\n\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"executed = %q, want a full run; out=%s\", got, out)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tif run.Carry == nil || run.Carry.Used || !strings.Contains(run.Carry.Reason, tc.reason) {\n\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"carry = %+v, want unused with a reason naming %q\", run.Carry, tc.reason)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t})\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryRootChangeRunsEverythingUnlessSelfContained(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tf.baseline()\n\tf.write(\"root.txt\", \"edited\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"root\")\n\tf.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a,b\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"root change executed %q, want everything\", got)\n\t}\n\n\tg := newCarryFixture(t, `{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"modules\":{\"b\":{\"selfContained\":true}}}`)\n\tg.baseline()\n\tg.write(\"root.txt\", \"edited\\n\")\n\tg.commit(\"root\")\n\t_, _, run := g.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif got := strings.Join(g.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"root change executed %q, want only the module that is not self-contained\", got)\n\t}\n\tif checkOf(run, \"b\").CarriedFrom == \"\" {\n\t\tt.Fatal(\"the self-contained module must be carried\")\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestChangedScopeHonoursSelfContainedModules(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, `{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"modules\":{\"b\":{\"selfContained\":true}}}`)\n\tf.write(\"root.txt\", \"edited\\n\")\n\t_, out := runValidate(t, f.root, \"--changed-from\", \"HEAD\", \"--explain\")\n\tif !strings.Contains(out, \"+ a: root-level change outside any module: root.txt\") || !strings.Contains(out, \"- b: not affected\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"self-contained module must not be widened by a root change: %s\", out)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryRecordFileIsNotAChange(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tf.baseline()\n\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif len(f.ran()) != 0 || run.Counts.Carried != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"executed=%v counts=%+v carry=%+v, want everything carried when only the record changed\", f.ran(), run.Counts, run.Carry)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarrySelectsAModuleWithoutAPriorResult(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tf.baseline()\n\traw, _ := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(f.root, carryResults))\n\tvar run map[string]any\n\t_ = json.Unmarshal(raw, &run)\n\tchecks := run[\"checks\"].([]any)\n\tkept := []any{}\n\tfor _, c := range checks {\n\t\tif c.(map[string]any)[\"module\"] != \"b\" {\n\t\t\tkept = append(kept, c)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\trun[\"checks\"] = kept\n\tout, _ := json.Marshal(run)\n\tf.write(carryResults, string(out))\n\tf.commit(\"base without b\")\n\t_, _, result := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"b\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"executed = %q, want only the module without a prior result\", got)\n\t}\n\tif !strings.Contains(result.Carry.Selected[\"b\"], \"no prior result\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"selection reason = %q\", result.Carry.Selected[\"b\"])\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryRefusesAFilteredBase(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tif code, out, _ := f.validate(\"--module\", \"a\"); code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tf.commit(\"partial record\")\n\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif run.Carry == nil || run.Carry.Used || !strings.Contains(run.Carry.Reason, \"filter\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"carry = %+v, want a filtered base refused\", run.Carry)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryRefusesIncompatibleFlags(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tfor _, extra := range [][]string{{\"--module\", \"a\"}, {\"--stack\", \"go\"}, {\"--changed-from\", \"HEAD\"}} {\n\t\targs := append([]string{\"--carry\"}, extra...)\n\t\tif code, out := runValidate(t, f.root, append(args, \"--json-out\", carryResults)...); code != 2 {\n\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"%v: exit=%d, want 2: %s\", extra, code, out)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif code, out := runValidate(t, f.root, \"--carry\", \"--json-out\", \"other.json\"); code != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"--carry with a non-canonical output: exit=%d, want 2: %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestCarryIgnoresDerivedAdministrativeRecords(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tf.baseline()\n\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/generation.json\", \"{\\\"generation\\\":2}\\n\")\n\tf.write(\".pose/state/project-state.md\", \"state\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"index and state\")\n\t_, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif len(f.ran()) != 0 || run.Counts.Carried != 2 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"executed=%v counts=%+v carry=%+v, want derived records not to count as changes\", f.ran(), run.Counts, run.Carry)\n\t}\n\t// A script stored in a class, and the validation matrix, stay material.\n\tf.write(\".pose/indexes/helper.sh\", \"echo\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"script in a class\")\n\tf.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif got := strings.Join(f.ran(), \",\"); got != \"a,b\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a non-record file in a class executed %q, want everything\", got)\n\t}\n}\n\n// A pass recorded over an uncommitted fix proves nothing about the committed\n// head; once the fix is dropped it must not be carried (review round 1, F1).\nfunc TestCarryRefusesABaseThatValidatedADirtyWorkingCopy(t *testing.T) {\n\tf := newCarryFixture(t, \"\")\n\tf.write(\"b/fail\", \"x\\n\")\n\tf.commit(\"b fails\")\n\tif err := os.Remove(filepath.Join(f.root, \"b\", \"fail\")); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif code, out, _ := f.validate(); code != 0 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the uncommitted fix should pass: exit=%d %s\", code, out)\n\t}\n\tf.git(\"add\", \"--\", carryResults)\n\tf.git(\"-c\", \"core.hooksPath=/dev/null\", \"commit\", \"-qm\", \"record over a dirty copy\")\n\tf.git(\"checkout\", \"--\", \"b/fail\")\n\tcode, _, run := f.validate(\"--carry\")\n\tif run.Carry == nil || run.Carry.Used || !strings.Contains(run.Carry.Reason, \"clean working copy\") {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"carry = %+v, want a dirty base refused\", run.Carry)\n\t}\n\tif code == 0 || checkOf(run, \"b\").Outcome != \"fail\" {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"exit=%d b=%+v, want b re-run and failing\", code, checkOf(run, \"b\"))\n\t}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"pose artifact-check --spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks --strict; pose suggest review --path pose-mcp; rg --files pose-mcp .pose/rules | rg '(AGENTS.md|backend-go|security.md|documentation-style|delivery-evidence|knowledge-governance)'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/rules/security.md .pose/rules/backend-go.md .pose/rules/documentation-style.md .pose/rules/delivery-evidence.md; sed -n '1,240p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go; rg -n 'materialWorktreeClean|materialTrackedUnchanged|func IsAdministrativePath' pose-mcp/internal; rg -n 'carried|carry|selfContained' POSE.md; rg -n 'carry|unchanged|validation' .pose/knowledge --glob '*.md' | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat .pose/rules/security.md .pose/rules/backend-go.md .pose/rules/documentation-style.md .pose/rules/delivery-evidence.md; sed -n '1,240p' pose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_scope.go; rg -n 'materialWorktreeClean|materialTrackedUnchanged|func IsAdministrativePath' pose-mcp/internal; rg -n 'carried|carry|selfContained' POSE.md; rg -n 'carry|unchanged|validation' .pose/knowledge --glob '*.md' | head -35\"","aggregated_output":"# Rule: Security\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for authentication, authorization, sensitive data, external integrations, dependencies, and attack surfaces.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Apply least privilege to resource and credential access.\n- Store secrets only in appropriate secret-management mechanisms, never in code.\n- Validate and sanitize external input for its execution context.\n- Keep personal and confidential data out of plaintext logs and metrics.\n- Evaluate new dependencies for maintenance, licensing, and known vulnerabilities.\n- Test both positive and negative authentication and authorization cases.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Committing credentials, tokens, keys, or secrets in any versioned artifact.\n- Disabling TLS or certificate verification without a formally documented mitigation.\n- Trusting client input for authorization decisions.\n- Executing dynamic commands without adequate validation and escaping.\n- Ignoring critical security alerts without an approved exception record.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run secret scanning on the changed diff or scope.\n- Run the module's applicable dependency vulnerability scanner.\n- Pass the relevant authentication and authorization tests.\n- Complete a review for sensitive-data exposure in logs and configuration.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Backend Go\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for HTTP handlers, domain services, persistence, concurrency, and integrations between Go services.\n\n## Required patterns\n\n- Handle and propagate errors with enough context for diagnosis.\n- Validate handler input and return HTTP status codes consistent with the contract.\n- Keep business rules in service or domain layers, not in transport code.\n- Honor `context.Context` for timeouts and cancellation.\n- Put data access behind clear interfaces that support testing.\n- Use structured logs without exposing sensitive data.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Ignoring error returns, including `_` for critical errors.\n- Using `panic` in ordinary request flows instead of controlled error handling.\n- Coupling handlers directly to concrete repository implementations without an abstraction.\n- Running unbounded or unpaginated queries in potentially large endpoints.\n- Introducing concurrency without adequate synchronization or race protection.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `go test ./...` in the affected scope.\n- Run `go test -race ./...` when concurrency changes.\n- Run `go vet ./...` without blocking findings.\n- Run the applicable Go lint check, such as `golangci-lint`, without critical errors.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Documentation Style\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide for process documentation, rules, specs, workflows, and operational instructions.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Write instructions in the imperative mood and begin them with action verbs.\n- Keep bullets short and limited to one idea.\n- Avoid duplicating sections with the same purpose across files.\n- Link to the single source of truth instead of copying its content.\n- Use `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` consistently.\n- State each instruction's scope explicitly to reduce ambiguity.\n\n## Examples: good and bad\n\n### Redundancy\n\n- **Good:** \"Update review criteria in `.pose/workflows/review.md` and reference that workflow from the root AGENTS file.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Repeat review criteria in AGENTS, the workflow, and every related spec.\"\n\n### Ambiguous reference\n\n- **Good:** \"Run the lint check described by the review workflow.\"\n- **Bad:** \"Run that standard validation before pushing.\"\n\n## Quick editorial checklist\n\n- Language is imperative and direct.\n- Bullets are short and non-overlapping.\n- Files do not duplicate sections.\n- `check`, `spec`, and `workflow` are used consistently.\n- References point to explicit files or paths.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Apply the most restrictive security, contract, and operational rule when domain rules conflict.\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence and explicit risk mitigation when speed conflicts with control.\n- Record the precedence decision and objective rationale in the review.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\n# Rule: Delivery Evidence\n\n## When to consult\n\nConsult this guide when writing or reviewing any document that **claims delivery,\ncompletion, or readiness**: status reports, handoffs, summaries, module READMEs,\ncurrent-state sections in specs or proposals, and messages that say something is complete.\n\n## Required conventions\n\n- Claim delivery only with attached, verifiable gate evidence: command and output\n  (`pose validate`, `go test`, `tsc`, `vitest`) or a link to the corresponding POSE report.\n- Use POSE lifecycle vocabulary: `draft`, `in-progress`, `done`, `blocked`,\n  `superseded`, or `abandoned`. Do not invent labels such as `completed` or `100% COMPLETE`.\n- Separate **implemented and verified** work from **planned or documented** work.\n  A plan describes intent; do not present that intent as current reality.\n- Reference the report or evidence that crossed the spec exit gate before using `done`.\n- Convert relative dates to absolute dates and stamp the verification date.\n- Declare exact source-tree artifact actions in the spec and reconcile them\n  against an immutable Git change set before claiming delivery.\n- Treat artifact declarations, Git observations and delivery verification as\n  separate evidence; none proves the others by itself.\n- For typed delivery targets, require a path from reconciled artifacts through\n  a production entrypoint to current structured validation results. A surface\n  requires reachability plus integration/e2e evidence; a composed capability\n  requires integration evidence.\n- Evidence answers for the component it ran in or for a component inside it: a\n  module-wide run covers its subtree, but a result from a directory inside a\n  component does not answer for the whole component. Coverage that rests only\n  on a containing module is reported as `inferred-coverage` — name it as such.\n\n## Blocking anti-patterns\n\n- Claiming `100% COMPLETE` or production readiness without a green\n  `pose validate --strict` for every affected module.\n- Publishing contradictory delivery documents for the same scope; reconcile them first.\n- Merging code with completion documentation before the applicable POSE checks pass.\n- Mixing aspiration and verified state in one paragraph without clear labels.\n\n## Minimum checks\n\n- Run `pose check --strict` for structure and spec status enums.\n- Run `pose validate --strict` for every module the document claims to deliver.\n- Run `pose lint-spec` when the document is a spec.\n- Run `pose artifact-check --strict` when the scope changes governed source-tree paths.\n- Run `pose surface-check --strict` for specs with `delivers` and `pose\n  roadmap-check --strict` before a roadmap claims completion.\n- `pose assess discover --if-stale --update-state` when closing specs to update completeness metrics, technical debt, and reports under `.pose/assessments/` and `.pose/state/`.\n\n## Precedence in multi-domain conflicts\n\n- Prefer verifiable check evidence over progress narratives when rules conflict.\n- When pressured to claim completion without a gate, record the real state\n  (`in-progress` or `blocked`) and the remaining exit conditions.\n\n## Recurrence traceability\n\n> Also apply: [.pose/rules/_base-recurrence.md](_base-recurrence.md)\npackage cli\n\n// Explainable changed-scope validation (spec pose-changed-scope-validation):\n// select the minimum safe set of modules from explicit Git revisions,\n// declared dependency edges and policy. Selection is deterministic for the\n// same base/head/config; every decision carries a reason and every\n// unselected check is recorded as skipped with a machine-readable reason.\n// Uncertainty always widens toward safe execution.\n\nimport (\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\tposemodel \"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"regexp\"\n\t\"sort\"\n\t\"strings\"\n)\n\n// safeGitRevRE rejects revisions that could be parsed as options or escape\n// expected forms (confinement: never pass untrusted flags to git).\nvar safeGitRevRE = regexp.MustCompile(`^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._/~^-]*$`)\n\ntype scopeModuleMeta struct {\n\tCriticality string   `json:\"criticality\"`\n\tDependsOn   []string `json:\"dependsOn\"`\n\t// SelfContained declares that the module's checks read only its own\n\t// directory and its declared dependencies, so a change outside every\n\t// module does not select it (spec pose-validate-carries-unchanged-checks R6).\n\tSelfContained bool `json:\"selfContained\"`\n}\n\ntype scopeMetadata struct {\n\tModules map[string]scopeModuleMeta `json:\"modules\"`\n}\n\nfunc loadScopeMetadata(root string) scopeMetadata {\n\tvar meta scopeMetadata\n\traw, err := os.ReadFile(filepath.Join(root, \".pose\", \"indexes\", \"module-metadata.json\"))\n\tif err == nil {\n\t\t_ = json.Unmarshal(raw, &meta)\n\t}\n\treturn meta\n}\n\n// scopeSelection maps selected module → deterministic selection reason.\ntype scopeSelection struct {\n\tBase, Head string\n\tChanged    []string\n\tSelected   map[string]string\n}\n\nfunc (s scopeSelection) rangeLabel() string {\n\tif s.Head == \"\" {\n\t\treturn s.Base + \"..worktree\"\n\t}\n\treturn s.Base + \"..\" + s.Head\n}\n\n// computeChangedScope resolves the selection. Modules are selected when they\n// contain changed files, depend (transitively) on a changed module, or policy\n// widens them (criticality high). A change outside every module selects\n// every module that does not declare itself self-contained — uncertainty\n// prefers safe execution. Excluded paths (the run's own canonical result) are\n// not changes.\nfunc computeChangedScope(root, base, head string, modules []validationModule, exclude ...string) (scopeSelection, error) {\n\tsel := scopeSelection{Base: base, Head: head, Selected: map[string]string{}}\n\tfor _, rev := range []string{base, head} {\n\t\tif rev != \"\" && !safeGitRevRE.MatchString(rev) {\n\t\t\treturn sel, fmt.Errorf(\"unsafe git revision: %q\", rev)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tgitArgs := []string{\"-C\", root, \"diff\", \"--name-only\", base}\n\tif head != \"\" {\n\t\tgitArgs = append(gitArgs, head)\n\t}\n\tgitArgs = append(gitArgs, \"--\")\n\tout, err := posemodel.RawGit(gitArgs...).Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn sel, fmt.Errorf(\"git diff %s failed (revision unknown?)\", sel.rangeLabel())\n\t}\n\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), \"\\n\") {\n\t\tif line != \"\" {\n\t\t\tsel.Changed = append(sel.Changed, filepath.ToSlash(line))\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif head == \"\" {\n\t\t// Worktree comparisons must also see untracked files — invisible new\n\t\t// code must never silently narrow validation (safe execution wins).\n\t\tif untracked, err := posemodel.RawHistoryGit(root, \"ls-files\", \"--others\", \"--exclude-standard\").Output(); err == nil {\n\t\t\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(untracked)), \"\\n\") {\n\t\t\t\tif line != \"\" {\n\t\t\t\t\tsel.Changed = append(sel.Changed, filepath.ToSlash(line))\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(exclude) > 0 {\n\t\texcluded := map[string]bool{}\n\t\tfor _, path := range exclude {\n\t\t\texcluded[filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(path))] = true\n\t\t}\n\t\tkept := sel.Changed[:0]\n\t\tfor _, file := range sel.Changed {\n\t\t\tif !excluded[file] {\n\t\t\t\tkept = append(kept, file)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tsel.Changed = kept\n\t}\n\tsort.Strings(sel.Changed)\n\n\tmeta := loadScopeMetadata(root)\n\tmoduleOf := func(file string) string {\n\t\tmatch := \"\"\n\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\tif file == m.Rel || strings.HasPrefix(file, m.Rel+\"/\") {\n\t\t\t\tif len(m.Rel) > len(match) {\n\t\t\t\t\tmatch = m.Rel\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn match\n\t}\n\tfor _, file := range sel.Changed {\n\t\tif m := moduleOf(file); m != \"\" {\n\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m]; !ok {\n\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m] = \"contains changed file: \" + file\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t} else {\n\t\t\t// Root-level or unmapped change: safe execution wins, except for\n\t\t\t// modules that declare their checks read nothing outside them.\n\t\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\t\tif meta.Modules[m.Rel].SelfContained {\n\t\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; !ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m.Rel] = \"root-level change outside any module: \" + file\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Policy widening: high-criticality modules always run.\n\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\tif meta.Modules[m.Rel].Criticality == \"high\" {\n\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; !ok {\n\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m.Rel] = \"policy: criticality high always runs\"\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\t// Transitive dependents of selected modules (declared edges only).\n\tfor changed := true; changed; {\n\t\tchanged = false\n\t\tfor _, m := range modules {\n\t\t\tif _, ok := sel.Selected[m.Rel]; ok {\n\t\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tfor _, dep := range meta.Modules[m.Rel].DependsOn {\n\t\t\t\tif reason, ok := sel.Selected[filepath.ToSlash(filepath.Clean(dep))]; ok {\n\t\t\t\t\tsel.Selected[m.Rel] = \"depends on selected module \" + dep + \" (\" + firstScopeReason(reason) + \")\"\n\t\t\t\t\tchanged = true\n\t\t\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t\t\t}\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn sel, nil\n}\n\n// firstScopeReason keeps chained dependency reasons short and readable.\nfunc firstScopeReason(reason string) string {\n\tif i := strings.Index(reason, \" (\"); i > 0 {\n\t\treturn reason[:i]\n\t}\n\treturn reason\n}\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_carry.go:101:\tif !materialWorktreeClean(root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1146:\tstartedClean := materialWorktreeClean(root)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1182:\t\tclean := materialWorktreeClean(root)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1422:\tendedClean := materialTrackedUnchanged(root)\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1680:// materialWorktreeClean reports whether the working copy equals HEAD in every\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1683:func materialWorktreeClean(root string) bool {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1713:// materialTrackedUnchanged reports whether no tracked file differs from HEAD\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate.go:1716:func materialTrackedUnchanged(root string) bool {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:17:\tif !materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:21:\tif !materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:25:\tif materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:30:\tif materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:35:\tif materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:50:\tif !materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:58:\tif materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) || materialTrackedUnchanged(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:68:\tif !materialTrackedUnchanged(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:72:\tif materialTrackedUnchanged(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:89:\tif materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) || materialTrackedUnchanged(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/cli/validate_material_worktree_test.go:96:\tif materialWorktreeClean(repo.root) {\npose-mcp/internal/pose/material_binding.go:36:func IsAdministrativePath(p string) bool {\n218:              [--changed-from rev [--changed-to rev] | --carry] [--jobs n] [--explain] [--emit-plan f]\n353:  **Changed scope:** `--changed-from <rev> [--changed-to <rev>]` selects the minimum safe module set deterministically — modules containing changed files (tracked and untracked), transitive dependents via `dependsOn` edges in [`module-metadata.json`](.pose/indexes/module-metadata.json), and policy widening (criticality `high` always runs). A change outside every module runs every module that does not declare `\"selfContained\": true` in its metadata (uncertainty prefers safe execution; the declaration states that the module's checks read only its own directory and its `dependsOn` modules); unselected checks are recorded as skipped with the selection reason and `--explain` prints every decision. Revisions are confined to a safe grammar; without the flags, full validation is unchanged.\n355:  **Carried checks:** `--carry --json-out <results_path>` refreshes the canonical result instead of rerunning the whole matrix. It uses the current canonical result as its base when the base is comparable: same schema, mode, matrix digest and `engine_version` (the engine version plus the VCS revision the binary was built from), unfiltered, not cancelled, recorded `material_clean` (it validated an unchanged working copy of its own head) with no unavailable material binding, with its `git_head` an ancestor of `HEAD`, and a clean material working copy now. It runs the modules the changed-scope rules select between the base head and `HEAD`; the canonical result file and the derived administrative records that material bindings ignore (data files that are still regular files in the derived-record directories under `.pose/`: indexes, results, state, assessments and review records; the validation matrix stays material) are not changes. A module whose checks are not all in the base also runs. Every other check keeps the base result, its outcome included, and records `carried_from` (the head it ran at, kept through chained carries) and `carried_generated_at`; the delivery index reads that head for the result, so evidence freshness judges the commit a carried check really observed. A carried failure still fails the run. Any unmet condition runs everything, and `carry.reason` says why. `carry` records the base, the number of changed files and each selected module's reason, and `counts.carried` the reused checks. `--carry` does not combine with `--module`, `--stack`, `--workspace`, `--root-only`, `--changed-from` or `--changed-to`.\n371:- `review-plan` / `review-check` / `closeout-check` — resolve a deterministic component-aware plan from governed metadata, typed overlays and a closed native-tool catalog, then validate immutable attempts against both scope and plan digests. Opt in explicitly with review-policy schema v2, `component_aware: true` and `component_aware_adopted_at`; preview the migration read-only with `pose review-plan <scope> --explain` before committing the policy. Completed attempts older than that component adoption date remain auditable, while open scopes require the new plan. `pose review record` is dry-run by default, accepts `--plan-digest` to reject drift and appends with `--apply`; recommended tools are never executed implicitly. Hierarchical closeout rolls approvals through `spec:`, `milestone:` and `roadmap:` scopes. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_plan`, `pose_closeout_state`. The JSON of `closeout-check` and `pose_closeout_state` types its blockers in `diagnostics` (code, domain, refs, satisfaction condition; text blockers the engine cannot type yet are carried as `opaque`) and its next action in `next_step`; readiness `waiting_on` entries carry a `code`, and auto-attest pendencies a `code`, `source` and `condition` (spec `pose-typed-producer-diagnostics`). The prose fields stay for people; consumers read the codes.\n372:- `review bundle` / `review attest` / `review auto-attest` / `review verify` / `review record` — opt-in fixed-point review (`review_bundles: true` plus an adoption date). Evaluation is a DAG, and each stage names the one before it: the implementation subject carries an `implementation_digest` over its classified content alone — not over change-set ids or refs, so a squash or rebase of the same content keeps its identity — and an observer of the implementation anchors there rather than on the bundle that will contain it. Sealed evidence records `subject_observation` as `observed`, `carried-forward` or `unknown`; a result naming no commit is unknown, never current. A change set's range observation reports how many commits `base..head` spans against how many it attributes, because paths resolved from trailers are attributed while the range between them is only provenance; it sits outside the sealed payload, so an unrelated commit never stales a review of content that did not move. A criterion is `mechanical` when it names evidence classes a registered check emits, and `judgment` otherwise: `auto-attest` answers the first kind from the sealed evidence and reports the second as pending, refusing `--apply` until a reviewer records a conclusion for each. A profile may declare `kind` explicitly to ask for judgment on a criterion a check also covers; it may not declare `mechanical` on one naming no evidence class. A component-scoped tool whose component the validation matrix declares runs no check (`replaceDefaultChecks` with an empty list) is planned with `producer_coverage: none` and may be disposed `not-used` with a reason, because nothing in the repository can emit what it asks for; everywhere else a required tool must still pass. Preparation hashes the semantic Intent, Requirements, Technical Plan and Decisions, attributed patch/tree content, consumed plan inputs, validation identities and child bundles. Tasks, execution logs, Final Report, lifecycle metadata, attestations and derived state remain outside the review digest but retain their own gates. `--seal` persists immutable JSON in the review-bundles directory under an `rvb-` identity; attestations are separate append-only JSON in review-attestations under an `rva-` identity. `auto-attest` extracts matching evidence from validation results and resolves tool dispositions deterministically. Semantic or source changes create a superseding bundle and typed delta; derived closeout changes do not cause rereview. Local review stays offline. Conductor may return an optional Ed25519 envelope only when its issuer/public-key pin is trusted by policy. Human plan output groups repeated path warnings and separates active required, recommended and completion-deferred tools; JSON retains full provenance. Sealing also records the governance contracts in force (`component-aware`, `review-bundles`, `evidence-vocabulary`) and the gates that decide the bundle (reservations, accepted-risk severities, criterion reuse); verification reads them from the bundle, and only `require_signed_attestations` is read live. Sealing warns about evidence produced against a commit other than the approved head. Sealing also records the implementation identity: principal, vendor, model, a digest of the sealing session's reference (never the reference) and whether it was `detected` from the session's agent markers or `declared` with `--seal --lead <vendor>[/<model>]`. The model comes from `--lead` or `routing.lead_models` and is `unknown` otherwise, which never establishes a model difference; markers of two vendors leave the vendor `unknown`. Resealing content a sealed bundle already holds returns that bundle with the identity it recorded, so a reviewer closing out never becomes the implementation; only a `--lead` naming another identity than the governing bundle's seals anew, and that seal names the bundle it replaces, so it governs whatever the seal instants say; a run compared with a replaced identity stops governing. The identity is sealed but is not content: a sealed bundle matches a preparation that differs from it only by the identity. An attestation's `passed` criterion must cite evidence the bundle contains, of a class the criterion accepts (any one of its `evidence_classes`), from the component it is about — evidence from a directory inside a component does not answer for the component. `--criterion ID|disposition|evidence|rationale` records `passed`, `not-applicable` (rationale required) or `finding` (names a recorded finding); `--finding ID|severity|disposition|action|evidence[|owner|rationale|review-by]` needs a severity and an action, and `accepted-risk` or `wont-fix` needs a sealed accepted severity, an owner, a rationale and a review date. Read-only MCP: `pose_review_bundle`. `pose_review_prepare` (read-only) returns the auto-attest draft and its pending judgment criteria; `pose_review_attest` (spec `pose-mcp-review-attest-signed-only`, `governance-write`) records an attestation over MCP only inside a trusted issuer's envelope, the proof `--envelope` verifies; its preview completes a draft for a sealed bundle (bundle digest, attested time, id and, when it names `confirmed_by`, the confirmation digest) and returns the exact bytes to sign. A human confirmation is disclosed as verified only when the signed authority claim names that human.\n376:  **Closeout review policy (schema 5):** an instance that adopts `pose adopt closeout-review-policy` decides with `milestone_review_required` and `roadmap_review_required` (`never`, `when-justified`, `always`) whether the close of a milestone or a roadmap owes its own review. `when-justified` requires it for a milestone with more than two distinct specs, and for a roadmap with more than one distinct spec and at least one ADR association (`adrs: [adr:<file>.md]` on the roadmap, `roadmaps: [roadmap:<slug>]` on the ADR, or a local Markdown link either way, read as CommonMark reads it). `always` requires it even for a one-member milestone, and neither the singleton inheritance nor `unreviewed_milestones` weakens it. Member reviews stay required; a review recorded on the scope anyway is verified as before and its rejection stands; a member or association that cannot be resolved never grants an exemption. A milestone that closes without its own review keeps its exit gate: the gate is carried into the review plan of its only member, or of its last member (the one sealed once every other member is terminal), and the close is blocked until an approved member review judged the gate as it reads now, or the milestone is reviewed itself. A member a transfer moved counts at its destination, and a milestone or roadmap named by `after:`, `consumes:` or a spec's `depends_on` satisfies the edge, and makes a dependent spec ready, only once closed under its project's adopted policy, not by its members' status; a done roadmap closed by its receipt keeps its milestones reachable again if their closure stops verifying. Under the adopted policy `pose close milestone:<roadmap>/<id>` and `pose close roadmap:<slug>` stay explicit in every mode and publish a receipt (`review-closeouts/<milestone|roadmap>/…json` inside `.pose/`) with `review: required|not-required`, the key, the value, `closeout-review@1` and the reason (`members=1, threshold>2`); the scope is terminal only with it. New instances adopt `never`/`never`; an existing policy keeps the legacy rule until it adopts, `pose setup` shows both, `pose doctor` lists open scopes that owe a review, and roadmaps closed before adoption are verified by the contract they were sealed under.\n380:- `artifact-check` — parses exact `### Artifacts` actions, resolves an explicit base/head range or `POSE-Spec: <slug>` commit trailers with safe structured Git arguments, and reports resolvability, existence, action mismatch, undeclared and orphan findings. A claim on a changelog fragment that `pose release prepare` archived resolves through the release manifest, which records the fragment's spec and digest; prepare never edits a spec. A file a later spec removes needs no longer exist for the earlier specs that declare it `created` or `modified`: their claim is satisfied — an edge `removed-by` in `delivery-integrity.json` — when the later spec declares `removed:` for the path, its attributed change sets contain a commit that deleted it (a rename is not a deletion), and every change the earlier spec made to the path — a merge, a root creation, a type change or a deletion included — is a Git ancestor of that commit; a change after the removal, a removal its spec never performed, a change set naming something other than an immutable commit, or history Git cannot read — a shallow clone included; replace refs and grafts are ignored — keeps the existence finding, and the finding says why. Commits modifying artifacts declared by a spec must carry a `POSE-Spec: <slug>` trailer in their commit message for `artifact-check` and `pose close` to attribute the changes. `pose report --change-from/--change-to` persists immutable change-set evidence; `pose index` projects claims, observations, reverse provenance and findings into `delivery-integrity.json`. The seven files `pose index` writes are one generation (spec `pose-index-generation-integrity`): their bytes are written under `generations/<id>/` in the indexes directory, the legacy names are published from the same bytes, and the descriptor `generation.json` there — SHA-256 of each file plus the digests of the inputs they were computed from — is published last and is the commit. A reader fixes the generation the descriptor names and checks each file against it: a set that does not verify (a file missing, replaced, from another generation, or a descriptor it cannot read) is `unavailable`, with the reason, never an empty or mixed result; a repository that never ran `pose index` since this contract keeps working and is reported `legacy-unversioned`, with no claim of coherence. A generated index edited by hand has no effect while the generation's copy exists (readers read the generation), and in a clone without the copy it makes the set unavailable; either way `pose index --verify` names the file. `pose index --verify` reads without writing and exits 1 unless the set is current, the legacy names are those of the generation and its inputs (specs, roadmaps, policy, module metadata, validation matrix) still hold. A failed publication leaves the previous generation in force and a retry converges on the same identity; publishers hold a generation lock in the indexes directory (it never waits: a second `pose index` is refused with `index-generation-busy` and writes nothing, and a lock whose holder is proven dead is recovered), the first publication of a repository is marked pending so one that did not commit reads as `unavailable` and not as a mixture of two runs, an operation that reads several indexes (the review plan) fixes one generation for all of them, and a commit whose durability could not be confirmed is reported as committed; retention keeps the current generation and the newest others up to three, never anything younger than ten minutes, and a retention failure is a warning, not a failed publication. Limits: Git history is read by the delivery graph but not pinned by the descriptor, and a clone without the git-ignored `generations/` directory verifies the legacy names against the descriptor. MCP: `pose_delivery_integrity`.\n385:- `state --attention` (specs `pose-obligation-projection`, `pose-state-attention`) — live, read-only answer to \"what is still owed, by whom, restricting which phase?\". Obligations are projected from the subsystems that own them — readiness (unmet `depends_on`, Definition of Ready, unexplained `blocked`), closeout and review of in-progress specs (an unapproved review, each judgment criterion a sealed bundle still owes, text blockers carried as `legacy-opaque`), start reconciliation when atomic start is adopted, and open follow-ups as advisory residual debt — never stored a second time. Project-level sources are projected too (spec `pose-attention-projects-every-source`), originating on `xref:<project>/project:<project>`: an open docs review mark (owed by the doc's owner until `pose docs-review resolve`), a capability mechanism with a stale trigger, a release newer than the newest verified one that is not yet verified, and an accepted-risk or follow-up finding past its review date in the latest legacy review record. Free-form investigation notes carry no state and are not a source. The render shows coverage first: a producer that failed, or action requests until adopted, makes the answer `INCOMPLETE`, so an empty group never reads as nothing owed, and a fresh install, once committed, reads `complete`. Then what needs `--actor` (or any person or role), then what restricts each phase (`start`, `execution`, `review`, `closeout`, `release`), then residual debt. `--json` returns the report — stable `obl-` ids, qualified refs, reason codes, conditions, recipients, effects, satisfaction, knowledge, waiting, a snapshot of revision/dirty tree/policy/contract/index digests, per-producer coverage — plus the `attention` grouping. MCP: `pose_obligations` returns the same ids from the same function. Attention is not a gate.\n650:names the matrix did not carry is reported as one `validation-check` fact per\n660:trailers also name other specs cannot be split by commit; its facts carry\n739:two directories are the contract — policy and profiles carry their own schema\n760:`agent:`/`human:` prefixes remain labels only. The attestation must carry a\n778:`closeout-check` print one assurance line and carry an `assurance` object in\n792:claim names that human. Records written before this carry no block and render\n821:An answer proof authorizes its answer only. New waiver, cancellation and supersession events carry `lifecycle_version: 1`. Under verified action policy they require an operation-bound SSHSIG (`pose-action-lifecycle`) or a trusted issuer's schema-2 action claim. MCP always requires this proof. Both supplied emitters must verify; human presence follows the current explicit channel scope.\n909:`pose delegate ls` lists attempts by their latest run with state, purpose, agent, label, turns, age and last step — for an interrupted run also its cause, whether a partial draft exists, the reset instant and the time left — and the runs in progress with the liveness of their process. `pose delegate cancel <run>` stops a run in progress; it is recorded `failed` with the cause `cancelled`, and its attempt stays consumed. `pose delegate agents` lists each agent with vendor, model, whether its CLI is on the path, the reset instant of its last recorded usage limit and the state routing reads: `available`, `limited until <instant>` (its last run hit a usage limit whose reset is ahead), `missing` (its CLI is not on the path), `unknown` (the run history could not be read whole) or `probe-failed` (its optional `probe` failed within its bound). Availability is read from the path and the recorded runs; only a configured probe runs anything. `pose delegate review|adjudicate|smoke --detach` starts the run as a process of its own, in a session of its own that survives the caller's terminal, and returns at once with the attempt (a run reference) and the path of its log; a run whose route must first wait for a usage-limit reset is not detached. `pose delegate wait <run> [--timeout <d>] [--json]` is a plain foreground process that ends when the run does and prints one line — run, state, decision, resumable flag, reset instant — with the exit code `0` approved, `10` changes requested or rejected, `20` failed (a completed run with no decision too), `30` interrupted, `40` stalled, `50` the wait timed out, and `2` for a usage error; every waiter is released, a run already ended returns at once, and killing a wait leaves the run running. The process running an attempt rewrites a heartbeat in its marker every 10 seconds; a run whose heartbeat is older than three intervals and whose process is provably gone is recorded `stalled` by the first reader, through an exclusive claim every reader then agrees on, and `ls` shows each running run's heartbeat age. `pose delegate watch [<run>|<label>]` shows an attempt as one task: the runs it recorded are replayed with a marker where a run resumed another and the interrupted run naming the run that continued it, then a running attempt is followed through the normalized, redacted events its process writes as they come, until the record is published; `--no-follow` prints what exists and exits, `-v` adds the tail of each tool's output (`--lines <n>`, default 12) and `--json` prints NDJSON. With no reference it follows the only running run; at a terminal it otherwise opens fzf with a preview of the run under the cursor refreshed every `--refresh <d>` (default 2s, 0 turns it off), or a numbered menu without fzf; outside a terminal an absent reference exits 2 and lists the candidates. Colours appear only on a terminal and follow `NO_COLOR`. A live event is redacted with the credentials and the session references the stream has declared so far; the events bound to the record are derived again from the whole transcript. `on_terminal` on an agent (or else on a purpose) is a command run once after a run ends, with only `POSE_DELEGATE_RUN` and `POSE_DELEGATE_STATE` in its environment, the run directory as working directory, its own process group and a bound (`timeout`, default 30s, at most 5m); its failure is recorded beside the run, shown by `ls`, and never changes the run's state. `pose delegate handoff <run> --to <agent|auto>` continues a run that stopped interrupted or failed, short of a conclusion, with another agent under the same attempt and its continuation budget, which is the `resume.max_resumes` and `max_wall_time` of the agent that opened the attempt (3 and 60 minutes by default) whichever agent continues it, so changing agents never widens it; a completed run, the same agent and a run already continued are refused, and a run handed off is no longer resumed. Its brief is the predecessor's generated brief followed by a labelled section: the predecessor's partial final message and the commands it ran with their exit status, paired by the call identifier the agent's stream records and otherwise left unknown rather than guessed, each with its digest, quoted so no line of it reads as a decision, bounded at 16 KiB with the cut disclosed, and an instruction to re-verify. Only the handoff run concludes. The record binds the base brief digest and the section digest in `handoff_digest`, names its predecessor in `hands_off_from`, and a signed proof verifies the section against the predecessor's record. `--to auto` routes as the purpose does, the stopped agent excluded and unavailable ones skipped. A foreground run or handoff that stops interrupted in a terminal offers waiting for its reset and resuming, handing off, or cancelling, which is offered even alone, and does nothing until answered; the stopping command's result, `ls --json` and `wait --json` carry the same ways forward as `next_actions`, each with its command, whether it is available now — judged by the planning its command runs, budget and wait bound included — and why not, and a `cancel` that leaves the run stopped. `watch` marks a handoff with `⇄` beside the `↻` of a resume. `pose delegate task <prompt-file|->` hands any task to another agent: the prompt goes on stdin in `--cd` (default: the current directory) with the agent's command for `--access read|sandboxed|write|full`; the default is `purposes.task.default_access` (read when unset), `full` is never a default and prints a warning naming what it disables. A task's template places `{add_dirs}`, `{no_network}` and `{outside_git}`, filled from the agent's `add_dir_args` (with `{dir}`), `no_network_args` and `outside_git_args`; `--add-dir`, `--no-network` and `--effort` that the agent's template cannot carry are refused with exit 2, and `--no-network` with `write` or `full` too. The final message goes to stdout and the agent's exit code is returned; `--json` replaces both with one document. A task runs in the real directory: containment bounds its time and output, not its filesystem. Its record (prompt, stream, error stream, normalized events, argv, removed variable names, exit code, label) is owner-only under the user's state directory (`$XDG_STATE_HOME/pose/delegate/`, where `XDG_STATE_HOME` counts only as an absolute path and `~/.local/state` is the default), or, with `--record`, in the `delegate-runs` directory of `.pose`. The state directory may be reached through a link of the user's own; resolved, it and every directory above it must belong to the user or to root and be writable by no group or other user unless sticky, and `pose`, `pose/delegate` and the project's directory below it must be real directories of the user's that no group or other user can write; a task refuses to record or read anywhere else and names the directory (on Windows only links are refused). `--detach` starts a task as the review purposes start a detached run and returns its attempt, its log and the place of its record; a prompt piped to `task -` or `resume --prompt -` is read before detaching and written to the detached run through a pipe, never a file, and a refusal before the attempt is reserved returns the foreground's document and exit code. A detached call keeps its log and hand-back in `.pose/review-private/supervisors`, which, with every directory up to the project root, must be the user's and writable by no group or other user, and whose root's ancestors must belong to the user or root and be writable by no one else unless sticky; the root is resolved once and only that resolved root is used, a caller whose current directory is gone is refused, and a project's private task history is keyed by its resolved root (records an earlier engine kept under a linked spelling are still read). A detached call believes a hand-back only once a piped prompt is through and a run reserved the attempt it names. A checkout that fails this can still run in the foreground; on Windows only links and non-directories are refused, and ACLs are not checked. A task is never evidence: no review reader reads it, `pose review attest --run` refuses it by name, and `wait` returns 0 when the agent succeeded, with no decision. `ls`, `watch`, `wait`, `cancel`, `resume` and `handoff` work on tasks; `resume` without `--prompt` sends a default continuation message, with the agent's `resume.commands` for the task's access, and `--agent auto` routes against the launching session. A task can also continue a session POSE never recorded, such as one a personal launcher opened, with `--session <id> --agent <name>`: the agent's resume command for the access runs with that session, the prompt optional, and the record says it continued a session without keeping it. `pose review dispatch`, `resume` and `status`, the aliases this surface deprecated, were removed: each exits 2 and names its `pose delegate` replacement.\n948:Reservations live in `.pose/review-attempts/`, final receipts link the run, and `.pose/review-run-ledger/` witnesses exact run bytes and transcript digests. Every publication uses the exclusive synced publisher. New runs carry `ledger_version: 1`, `attempt_id` and the optional reason; legacy schema-1 runs retain their original identities, with no invented reservation or signature. An incomplete reservation, missing witness/run/transcript, inconsistent receipt, unknown record or exhausted read budget is explicitly incomplete. Inspect it; no automatic deletion, truncation, repair or retry occurs. Upgrade writers sharing these directories together. Run continuity and authenticated provenance remain separate deliveries.\n956:**Hosted checks and the resident Git memo.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <command> [args...]` runs `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` or `roadmap-check` in the runtime of the worktree when one is running, compatible and willing, and standalone in this process otherwise; the exit code and the output are the command's, and `--envelope` prints one document (`pose.runtime.run/1`) with them and with `via` (`runtime` or `standalone`), the `fallback_reason`, the typed `outcome` (`completed`; `error` when the command ran and ended but its output (over 2 MiB) is not sent back, with the command's exit code; or `unknown` for a deadline or a lost answer), the epoch and the memo hits. The runtime refuses, before the command starts, one it does not host and a caller whose environment digest (the `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` and `CODEX` variables, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, the locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` and the user) differs from its own, so the caller runs standalone; a command that reached the runtime and whose answer did not come back whole, or whose `--timeout` passed, may have run and is never repeated standalone unasked: it exits 1 and says so. The runtime runs hosted commands as the user that started it, in its worktree, with the output of a non-terminal, in the admission lanes described below; a command still waiting for its slot when its `--timeout` passes is refused, not started, so the caller runs it standalone. A runtime also keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by that commit and the shallow state (256 MiB, least recently used first; not for a linked worktree): the second `review-check` of this repository took about 10 s against about 25 s standalone, with the same output. The runtime admits hosted commands in two lanes, each with its own slots and a bounded queue: `short` (`surface-check`, about 10 ms on this repository) and `heavy` (the other hosted commands; `roadmap-check <slug>` takes minutes here), 2 and 1 slots by default and queues of 16 and 4; `pose runtime start|serve` take `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` and `--memo-mib` (default 256). A short query is not queued behind a heavy check, and a command that finds every slot of its lane busy and the queue full is refused before it waits and runs standalone. `status` and the handshake carry, per lane, `<lane>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` and `_wait_ms_max` in `metrics`. A measurement of the runtime on a real repository, with its thresholds fixed before the numbers, is `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<file> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; its report is under `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Review rounds.** The rounds of a scope are its sealed bundles that a completed delegated review concluded on, in the order those runs ended; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` and `pose insights` show the round. From round 2, the generated brief (template `review-brief/2`; runs briefed with `review-brief/1` still verify) carries a `Previous round` section built from the ledger: every open finding with the identifier it keeps across rounds (`R<round>-<id>`), its severity, disposition and location, the previous run and its draft digest. The reviewer's draft is the fenced code block whose info string is `pose-draft`: JSON with `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` and `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), and from round 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) and `sweep`. A finding reported `partial` or `not-fixed` keeps its identifier in the next brief. A round-2-or-later draft that leaves an open finding unaccounted, calls one `fixed` without evidence or states no sweep is recorded with `draft_problems`, `wait` reports it as `invalid`, and it is never accepted as an approval. With `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (the default) a round resumes the session the previous round ended with, through the agent's resume command; it opens a new session, recording why, when the agent or its vendor changes, the chain of drafts is broken, that session cannot be resumed, or the round reaches `routing.round_threshold` (5 by default), where `verify` also warns that the rounds are not converging. `cold-session` opens a new session every round. Each run records its `round` and `thread`; `pose insights` reports, per scope, findings per round, rounds to approval, and how prior findings were accounted for in continued and in cold rounds. A previous round whose record is not intact makes the brief refuse.\n958:**Delegated runs over MCP.** `pose_delegate_start` (review, adjudicate or smoke), `pose_delegate_status`, `pose_delegate_wait`, `pose_delegate_resume` and `pose_delegate_handoff` run `pose delegate … --json` in the project the server resolved for the call, so every plan, refusal and run is the CLI's: a start, resume or handoff is always detached and returns once the run has reserved its attempt (without `apply` it previews), a status never blocks, and a wait blocks at most its `timeout_seconds` (300 at most), returning `still-running` when the bound passes. Refusals carry the same `reason_code` as the CLI's error document (`rerun-without-reason`, `budget-exhausted`, `containment-incompatible`, `agent-unavailable`, `no-agent`), and results carry the normalized summary only: no transcript, prompt, session reference or environment value. `pose delegate resume` and `handoff` take `--detach` as the purposes do. A task is not started over MCP.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-27-handoff-delegation-mcp-round5.md:23:Independent round 5 reviewed implementation f0514d01404e7bed0cfc602f2db065f3e0c6289e of spec pose-delegation-mcp. The maintainer-authorized reviewer agent:independent-gpt-6.1-sol-review declares Codex/OpenAI, another vendor than implementer Claude/Anthropic. Identity is declared; this instance uses unsigned attestations. Last reviewed: 2026-10-27. Native global strict validation passed 158/158 checks at that implementation head.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-27-handoff-delegation-mcp-round5.md:45:Spec: .pose/specs/2026-10-26-pose-delegation-mcp.md. Consumed knowledge: knowledge:delegation-observation-round4, knowledge:delegation-routing-round3 and knowledge:delegated-rounds-legacy-status. Canonical validation: .pose/results/delivery-validation.json. Local probes, seven mutations and reports: .review-work/round5/ (deliberately untracked). Native bundle and attestation IDs are recorded in the spec Final Report after closeout.\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-09-24-handoff-multirepo-review-continuation.md:12:  commands: [\"pose review verify spec:pose-agent-project-context\", \"pose closeout-check roadmap:pose-multirepo-foundation\", \"pose validate --strict --module pose-mcp --json .pose/results/delivery-validation.json --report\"]\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:253:  `pose-changed-scope-validation`.\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-07-19-handoff-contract-baseline-handoff.md:256:  `pose-monorepo-validation-recipes`.\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:45:separate mechanism carrying separate self-referential data (JSON policy\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:77:  `.pose/adr/2026-07-19-mcp-conductor-harness-trust-boundary-for-safe-validation-orchestration.md`\n.pose/knowledge/archive/2026-08-15-decision-log-self-referential-policy-template-contamination.md:127:  `.pose/adr/2026-07-19-mcp-conductor-harness-trust-boundary-for-safe-validation-orchestration.md`\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-14-decision-log-escalation-validate-native.md:12:  commands: [\"pose recurrence-check --tolerant --window-days 30 --threshold 3\", \"pose stats workflows --since-days 30\", \"pose stats tasks --since-days 30 --json\", \"pose validate --strict --module pose-mcp --json .pose/results/delivery-validation.json --report\"]\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-14-decision-log-escalation-validate-native.md:30:Commit `3177e80` excludes `.qwen` from validation discovery and adds a\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-14-decision-log-escalation-validate-native.md:32:Strict module validation now passes and its canonical JSON is bound to both\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-13-decision-log-action-answer-proof-and-historical-time.md:26:A stored label is not a cryptographic result. A signature authenticates signed bytes; it does not make unsigned presence metadata authoritative. Acceptance time and present-day trust are separate inputs. Legacy second-resolution timestamps describe an interval, so historical validation uses its bounded precision rather than pretending a nanosecond clock was retained.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-02-handoff-roadmap-v7-independent-outcomes-20261102.md:24:Fresh CLI build, focused CLI/signature/MCP/presence tests, all quickstart chapters and install/6.2.0-upgrade journeys pass. Supplied strict validation has 184 passing checks at HEAD; full matrix was inspected, not rerun. All three prose milestone gates and their current dependency order were independently reviewed.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-11-02-handoff-agency-readiness-outcome-review.md:27:The earlier composed-validation blocker no longer reproduces. Fresh full\n.pose/knowledge/2026-08-16-decision-log-module-metadata-discovery-invalidates-review-provenance.md:44:deliberately left unchanged (Decision 2 in the spec above): narrowing the\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-20-handoff-pose-b07-implementation.md:29:`../results/b07-validation.json`.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-20-handoff-pose-b07-implementation.md:34:2. The unused expired Actions waiver was removed separately in d957b4f. No pin or enforcement change. Reconciled full suite, 11/11 mutations and native 141/141 validation passed before the latest three review fixes; regenerate final evidence at the configured .pose/results/delivery-validation.json.\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-28-handoff-delegated-conclusions-round4.md:28:The Unix backend propagates flock errors unchanged. A synthetic overlay returning syscall.EOPNOTSUPP, with only the wait bound reduced from two minutes to 200 ms, reached the deadline in 201 ms and reported another conclusion holding the lock. It published zero proofs and attestations. This is injected error-path evidence, not a claim that the local filesystem lacks flock. Evidence: .review-work/round4/permanent.log, permanent-overlay.json, permanent_lock.go and permanent_wait.go (local, untracked).\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:32:when the spec closes, and retired when the ADR and `docs-site/docs/cli.md` carry\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:36:`pose check --strict` and `pose knowledge-check --strict` are the validation this\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:113:Three persistence paths exist, and they carry different things — worth keeping\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:118:| `.pose/results/*.json` (validation result) | each check's own captured `Output` (tail, secrets redacted), outcome, duration, exit code | only if we change what we capture from the child, not how we print |\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:335:   built from the printed run, so it moves with any restyle, while the validation\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:397:  restyling moves it; the validation JSON moves only if capture changes. Expected\n.pose/knowledge/2026-09-12-note-cli-output-design-taxonomy.md:419:- `pose-mcp/internal/pose/delivery_integrity.go` — findings already carry\n.pose/knowledge/2026-10-13-decision-log-review-assurance-requires-cryptographic-proof.md:22:Verify the stored signature and exact supplied bundle binding inside the shared authority checker. Let the declared path retain its existing signature gate. Keep criteria and decision validation outside authority verification, so a valid proof does not become invalid identity merely because approval fails.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
